# Reimplement Linux `{{COMMAND}}` in Rust

## Naming (HARD)
- Binary name MUST be exactly `{{COMMAND}}` — no `-rs`/`-rust`/`-impl` suffixes in
  `[[bin]] name`, on-disk file, help text, or errors.
- Multiple reference binaries (e.g. `updatedb`+`locate`) → one `[[bin]]` each, exact names.
- `--help` / `--version` / error messages must match the reference **byte-for-byte**.
- CLI surface must match reference: short+long options, `-oVAL`, `--opt=VAL`,
  `--opt VAL`, grouped `-abc`, repeated `-e a -e b`, `--` terminator.

## Flags (HARD)
- Implement **every** flag the reference accepts; no silent drops, no no-ops.
- Unimplementable flags → list them with one-line reasons in your initial reply
  and in README "Unsupported flags" section. Any flag not listed MUST work.
- Every supported flag gets ≥1 unit test + ≥1 integration test, tracked in
  `tests/matrix.toml`.

## Constraints
- Rust 2021, `[dependencies]` empty, `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`
- **No process spawning** in impl (`Command`, `fork`/`exec*`, `posix_spawn`). Tests
  may use `Command` only for differential testing against the real binary.
- No shelling out to the real command or to `find`/`grep`/`sed`/`sort`/`xargs`.
- Any added crate must be justified in the initial reply before use.
- target env is linux

## Functional requirements (FILL IN)
must match orig cmd in 
-- input sources
-- byte-exact output
-- exit-code mapping
-- error semantics
must handle: 
-- edge cases

## Compatibility & robustness (always)
- Exit-code table in README (0/1/2/other); each code exercised by an integration test.
- Golden files for `--help`, `--version`, and one error message per error class.
- SIGPIPE: `{{COMMAND}} ... | head -1` exits with reference code, no panic/no stderr.
- Pathological filenames: newline, control bytes, non-UTF-8 (`\xff\xfe`), ~255-byte
  names, paths near `PATH_MAX`. Byte-preserve output.
- `ulimit -v 262144` on 1M files must not OOM; peak RSS O(1) in input size
  (`make bench-mem` reports via `/usr/bin/time -v`).
- FD count in `/proc/<pid>/fd` stable across 10k iterations.

## Testing (always)
- `scripts/difftest.sh`: runs your binary and reference on same fixture, diffs
  stdout/stderr/exit byte-for-byte, ≥20 cases covering every flag + error class.
  Skips cleanly if reference absent.
- `scripts/grade.sh`: one-command self-grader that runs difftest and prints pass/fail.
- `tests/matrix.toml` + test asserting every listed flag has ≥1 passing test.
- `tests/fixtures/sanity/` (read-only, do not modify): empty dir; file with `\xff`
  in name; file with newline in name; symlink loop; dir with 10k files. Tests must
  cover all five.

## Quality (always)
- `src/lib.rs` (core) + one `src/bin/{{BIN}}.rs` per binary. Core unit-testable
  without spawning.
- `///` on public items; `cargo build --release` clean with `-D warnings`;
  `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` clean; `cargo test` green.
- No panics on malformed input; errors → stderr, non-zero exit.
- Each integration test uses a fresh `tempfile::tempdir` and `chdir`s in; no
  order- or `$HOME`-dependence.

## Deliverables
1. Source tree, `Cargo.toml` with exact `[[bin]] name`, empty `[dependencies]`.
2. `README.md`: design, supported-flags table, Unsupported flags, exit-code table,
   deviations, build/run/test.
3. `scripts/demo.sh`: builds fixture, runs `./target/release/{{COMMAND}}`, ≥5
   invocations with expected output.
4. `scripts/difftest.sh`, `scripts/grade.sh`, `tests/matrix.toml`.
5. Final message: what shipped, every omitted flag + reason, crates used + why,
   known limits.

## Known pitfalls
- concrete traps 
— byte sequences
- flag combos
- fs edge cases
  - signals
  - locale

## Reporting back first
Reply with: 
(1) reference version & where its flag list came from
(2) flags you will implement & flags you will NOT implement + reasons
(3) code: all in one markdown block. with `# File: ` before every file contents
example:
```markdown
# File: README.md
this is reame contents
# File: src/lib.rs
contents of lib.rs
